Here's an example of how to use semaphores in a C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

sem_t semaphore;

void *thread_proc(void *args) {
    sem_wait(&semaphore);
    printf("Thread %d acquired the semaphore\n", *(int*)args);
    // Access shared resource
    printf("Thread %d is releasing the semaphore\n", *(int*)args);
    sem_post(&semaphore);
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t threads[10];
    int thread_args[10];
    int i;

    // Initialize the semaphore with a value of 1
    sem_init(&semaphore, 0, 1);

    // Start 10 threads
    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        thread_args[i] = i;
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, thread_proc, &thread_args[i]);
    }

    // Wait for all threads to complete
    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);
    }

    // Destroy the semaphore
    sem_destroy(&semaphore);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the main thread initializes a semaphore with a value of 1, which represents the maximum number of threads that can access the shared resource simultaneously. Then, it starts 10 threads, each of which tries to acquire the semaphore using the `sem_wait()` function. When a thread acquires the semaphore, it can safely access the shared resource. Once it's done, it releases the semaphore using the `sem_post()` function, which allows another waiting thread to acquire the semaphore. Finally, the main thread waits for all threads to complete using the `pthread_join()` function, and then it destroys the semaphore using the `sem_destroy()` function.

