This code snippet is an example of inter-process communication using memory barriers. 

In this example, `foo()` and `bar()` are two independent functions running on separate processors or threads. `a` and `b` are two shared variables that can be accessed by both functions.

`foo()` sets the value of `a` to 1, then calls `smp_mb()` which is a memory barrier. A memory barrier is a synchronization method that ensures that the processor or thread executing `foo()` completes all memory operations before continuing to the next instruction. In this case, the memory barrier ensures that the value of `a` is visible to other processors or threads.

After the memory barrier, `foo()` sets the value of `b` to 1.

`bar()` waits in a busy loop until the value of `b` becomes 1. Once `b` is 1, `bar()` checks that the value of `a` is also 1 using `assert()`. If `a` is not 1, the assertion will fail and the program will terminate.

This code snippet demonstrates how memory barriers can be used to synchronize shared variables between multiple processors or threads. By using memory barriers, we can ensure that changes to shared variables made by one processor or thread are visible to all other processors or threads, and therefore ensure correct program behavior.

