Here's a composable function in Kotlin that generates a layout with two `Text` views, one `TextField`, and one `Button`:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyLayout() {
    Column(
        modifier = Modifier
            .fillMaxWidth()
            .padding(16.dp)
    ) {
        Text(
            text = "Hello",
            fontSize = 24.sp,
            fontWeight = FontWeight.Bold
        )
        Text(
            text = "World",
            fontSize = 24.sp,
            fontWeight = FontWeight.Bold
        )
        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(16.dp))
        var textFieldValue by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
        TextField(
            value = textFieldValue,
            onValueChange = { textFieldValue = it },
            label = { Text("Enter text") }
        )
        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(16.dp))
        Button(onClick = {}) {
            Text(text = "Submit")
        }
    }
}
```

You can use this function in your `setContent` function to display the layout:

```kotlin
setContent {
    MyLayout()
}
```

This will display a layout with two `Text` views ("Hello" and "World"), a `TextField`, and a `Button`. When the button is clicked, it will execute the `onClick` lambda that you specify.

Here's an example composable function in Kotlin that shows a `LinearProgressIndicator` inside a `Column` layout. The progress of the indicator increases every second:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyLayout() {
    var progress by remember { mutableStateOf(0.0f) }

    Column(
        modifier = Modifier
            .fillMaxWidth()
            .padding(16.dp),
        horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally
    ) {
        LinearProgressIndicator(progress)
        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(32.dp))
        Text(text = "Progress: ${progress * 100}%", fontSize = 20.sp)
    }

    LaunchedEffect(Unit) {
        while (true) {
            delay(1000L)
            progress += 0.1f // increase the progress by 10% every second
            if (progress >= 1.0f) {
                progress = 0.0f // reset the progress to 0 when it reaches 100%
            }
        }
    }
}
```

You can use this function in your `setContent` function to display the layout:

```kotlin
setContent {
    MyLayout()
}
```

This will display a layout with a `LinearProgressIndicator` and a `Text` view that shows the progress percentage. The progress of the indicator will increase by 10% every second, and it will reset to 0% when it reaches 100%.

In Jetpack Compose, you can easily update the UI in real-time by using state management. Here's an example of how you can create a composable function that updates the UI in real-time:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyLayout() {
    var value by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }

    Column(
        modifier = Modifier
            .fillMaxWidth()
            .padding(16.dp),
        horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally
    ) {
        Text(text = "Value: $value", fontSize = 24.sp)
        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(16.dp))
        Button(onClick = { value++ }) {
            Text(text = "Increment")
        }
    }

    LaunchedEffect(value) {
        while (true) {
            delay(1000L)
            value += 1 // increment the value every second
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `mutableStateOf` function to create a state variable called `value` that defaults to 0. We then display the value in a `Text` view and provide a button that increments the value when clicked.

We also use the `LaunchedEffect` composable function to create a coroutine that runs in the background and updates the `value` variable every second. Whenever `value` changes, the composable function will recompose, and the UI will update in real-time.

You can use this function in your `setContent` function to display the layout:

```kotlin
setContent {
    MyLayout()
}
```

This will display a layout with a `Text` view that shows the current value and a `Button` that increments the value. The value will be incremented every second, and the UI will update in real-time.

In Jetpack Compose, you can reload values of composables in real-time by using `remember` and `LaunchedEffect`. Here's an example of how you can create a composable function that reloads its value in real-time:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyLayout() {
    var value by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }

    Column(
        modifier = Modifier
            .fillMaxWidth()
            .padding(16.dp),
        horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally
    ) {
        Text(text = "Value: $value", fontSize = 24.sp)
        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(16.dp))
        Button(onClick = { value++ }) {
            Text(text = "Increment")
        }
    }

    LaunchedEffect(true) {
        while (true) {
            delay(1000L)
            value = (0..100).random() // generate a random number between 0 and 100
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `mutableStateOf` function to create a state variable called `value` that defaults to 0. We then display the value in a `Text` view and provide a button that increments the value when clicked.

We also use the `LaunchedEffect` composable function to create a coroutine that runs in the background and generates a random number between 0 and 100 every second. Whenever the `value` variable changes, the composable function will recompose, and the UI will reload in real-time.

You can use this function in your `setContent` function to display the layout:

```kotlin
setContent {
    MyLayout()
}
```

This will display a layout with a `Text` view that shows the current value and a `Button` that increments the value. The value will be replaced with a random number between 0 and 100 every second, and the UI will reload in real-time.

